            <title>[LU-20926] e2fsprogs: resize2fs sparse_super2/block map and mke2fs packed_meta_blocks bugs</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20926</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="error"&gt;&amp;#91;Claude&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt; Two resize2fs bugs found while prototyping metadata migration for &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20924" title="hybrid ldiskfs: metadata not allocated in IOPS groups" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-20924"&gt;LU-20924&lt;/a&gt;.  Both are in upstream e2fsprogs as well as the Lustre branch, and are independent of hybrid/IOPS support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name="sparsesuper2growfreesaninuseblock"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;sparse_super2 grow frees an in-use block&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a sparse_super2 filesystem is grown, the backup superblock and group descriptors move from the old last group to the new last group, and &lt;tt&gt;clear_sparse_super2_last_group()&lt;/tt&gt; frees the old copy.  It frees one block too many after the descriptors, because the count returned by &lt;tt&gt;ext2fs_super_and_bgd_loc2()&lt;/tt&gt; includes the superblock.  If that block is in use, it can later be allocated a second time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also does not mark the block bitmap dirty, so if the bitmaps were already written by &lt;tt&gt;move_itables()&lt;/tt&gt; the old backup is never freed and the group free block count no longer matches the bitmap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fix: free only the backup blocks and mark the bitmap dirty.  Fixes: 65c6c3e06f72 ("Add support for new compat feature "sparse_super2"").  New regression test &lt;tt&gt;r_sparse_super2_grow&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name="blockmapsortbreaksonfilesystemsover8TiB"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;block map sort breaks on filesystems over 8TiB&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;extent_cmp()&lt;/tt&gt; in resize/extent.c returns the difference of two &lt;tt&gt;__u64&lt;/tt&gt; block numbers as an &lt;tt&gt;int&lt;/tt&gt;, so blocks 2^31 or more apart (filesystems over 8TiB with 4KiB blocks) sort in the wrong order once the map is unsorted, and &lt;tt&gt;ext2fs_extent_translate()&lt;/tt&gt; then misses entries.  This dates back to the original resize2fs, but resize2fs adds blocks in order, so the sort is not normally used.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fix: compare the block numbers instead of subtracting them.  New test_extent case.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both fixes are to be submitted to linux-ext4 as well as tools/e2fsprogs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;a name="mke2fspackedmetablockswithonegroupperflexgroup"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;mke2fs packed_meta_blocks with one group per flex group&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;mke2fs -t ext4 -b 4096 -g 8192 -G 1 -O sparse_super2,flex_bg,^resize_inode -E packed_meta_blocks=1,num_backup_sb=1&lt;/tt&gt; on a 128MiB image creates an inconsistent filesystem: e2fsck reports group free block counts off by 66 blocks per group, and the kernel returns EIO/ENOSPC on mkdir.  It does not need &lt;tt&gt;-E iops&lt;/tt&gt;; &lt;tt&gt;-G 2&lt;/tt&gt; is clean.  Fix and regression test to follow with the two resize2fs fixes.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
